  "notes": "Boom-bap drums inside a g-funk house, and the research's own phrase for the kit is \"hardest snare on the label\" — which is why `velocityTiers.accent` is lifted to 112–127 and `main` to 84–98, the loudest backbeat in this batch. Everything else is subtraction: kick on 1 and 3 only (`densityPerBar` 2–3, `syncopation` 0.16, `offbeat8thShare` 0.1), hats simple closed 8ths so the syllabic delivery reads, one perc lane, no pad. ⛔ **`chords.impliedByRiff` is set and it is a description, not an exemption.** The research: 2-bar loop, one lead voice (organ or filtered horn stab), no pad, \"static single-chord vamps are common — harmony deliberately minimal to leave the mid-range to the vocal\". One chord is the part. What carries the 55-distinct-outputs floor is `chordDurationBeats` 2–14 against a `syncopated_cell` harmonic rhythm — how long the loop sits, not what it sits on — plus a voicing register spanning 45–79. ⚠ **The `i–VII` and `i–iv` families are INFERRED**, not researched: the research names only the static form. They carry weight 2 against the static `i`'s 5 and are the first thing to delete if this ever sounds busier than the records. ⚠ `confidence` is `medium` per the research's own rating, which marks the per-track harmonic detail [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The bass is `mirror_kick` because the research says single-note-per-chord and kick-adjacent; `followRootsProb` is 0.9 rather than 1.0 and the span is 18 semitones so the line still has a reachable pool.",
